\title{Notes on formulating discrete-induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{These are the accompanying mathematical notes for the experimental research
project FDIP. FDIP is read “F-dip,” for discrete-induced physics: a physics that
starts from finitely many points and exact algebra and induces geometry, time and
matter from them. It is also a coined acronym for “Formulating Discrete-Induced
Physics.”}

\p{The notes build the mathematics that a discrete-induced physics would need:
fields on finitely many points and the operators they become, the product rules
those operators obey, the finite groups that act on them and the Clifford algebras
that carry metric and spin, each layer connected to the next by an explicit map or
limit. Induced names that requirement, a construction
of that kind rather than a continuum sampled on a grid. Inspired by Wilson's hypothesis that
background independence is a hidden assumption
\citek{wilson2025background}, and with Mach's principle in mind, the notes keep
these constructions compatible with a background-dependent physics rather than
requiring one: each construction is asked what a declared background would add,
what map would carry it and whether it admits such an indexing, so compatibility
is what gets tested. The mathematics developed in the
notes is strongly physics-motivated, but the interest and the focus are the
mathematics that answers the challenges: no physical theory is claimed, and where
the notes include a falsifiable proposal for one, it is an example of applying the
tools developed here.}

